ny integer \\(b\\).  \n   Using \\(a=p\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   p^{k_{p}}\\mid b^{p}-f(b)^{\\,p^{k_{p}}}.\n   \\]\n   Reducing modulo \\(p\\) and using \\(x^{p}\\equiv x\\pmod p\\) gives  \n   \\[\n   p\\mid b-f(b).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Thus for every prime \\(p\\) with \\(f(p)>1\\) we obtain the congruence  \n   \\[\n   f(b)\\equiv b\\pmod p\\qquad\\forall\\,b\\in\\mathbb N .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **All primes satisfy \\(f(p)=p\\).**  \n   Suppose some prime \\(p\\) has \\(k_{p}\\ge2\\).  \n   Choose an integer \\(b\\) that is a primitive root modulo \\(p^{k_{p}}\\); such a \\(b\\) exists because the multiplicative group \\((\\mathbb Z/p^{k_{p}}\\mathbb Z)^{\\times}\\) is cyclic.  \n   For this \\(b\\) the order of \\(b\\) modulo \\(p^{k_{p}}\\) is \\(\\varphi(p^{k_{p}})=p^{k_{p}-1}(p-1)\\).  \n   From (2) we have \\(p\\mid b-f(b)\\), so \\(b\\not\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{k_{p}}}\\).  \n   Consequently \\(b^{p}\\not\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{k_{p}}}\\); hence  \n   \\[\n   p^{k_{p}}\\nmid b^{p}-1.\n   \\]\n   Taking \\(f(b)=1\\) (which is allowed because \\(f(1)=1\\)) the divisibility condition for \\(a=p,\\;b\\) reads  \n   \\[\n   p^{k_{p}}\\mid b^{p}-1,\n   \\]\n   a contradiction.  \n   Therefore no prime can have \\(k_{p}\\ge2\\); thus for every prime  \n   \\[\n   f(p)=p .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Consequences of (2) with (3).**  \n   Since (2) holds for **every** prime (by (3) each prime satisfies \\(f(p)>1\\)), we obtain for all primes \\(p\\) and all \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   f(b)\\equiv b\\pmod p .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Hence for any integer \\(n\\) and any prime \\(p\\mid n\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\equiv n\\pmod p .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Prime‑power exponents in \\(f(n)\\).**  \n   Write the prime factorisation \\(n=\\prod p^{e_{p}}\\).  \n   Let \\(v_{p}(x)\\) denote the exponent of \\(p\\) in \\(x\\).  \n   From (5) we get \\(v_{p}(f(n))\\ge e_{p}\\).  \n   Apply the defining condition with \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=p\\) (where \\(p\\mid n\\)):\n   \\[