sume is not constant), there exists some b such that f(b) = 1. Actually, they might be using the fact that f(1)=1, and they can set b=1. But they need b to be a primitive root modulo p^{k_p}, which is >1. So b cannot be 1. So they cannot set f(b)=1 for that b unless they prove that f(b)=1 for that b. So this step is suspect.\n\nLet's examine more carefully: The condition is that for all a,b, f(a) | b^a - f(b)^{f(a)}. They have a = p, b = some primitive root modulo p^{k_p}. The condition becomes p^{k_p} | b^p - f(b)^{p^{k_p}}. They want to derive a contradiction by showing that the RHS cannot be divisible by p^{k_p} if f(b) = 1. But they cannot assume f(b) = 1. They need to consider the actual f(b) value. They could try to argue that f(b) must be 1 because otherwise the RHS would be too large? Let's see.\n\nIf f(b) > 1, then f(b) is some integer dividing b^p - something. But we need to examine the divisibility condition more generally.\n\nAlternatively, they could try to use the congruence (2) that f(b) ≡ b (mod p). Since p divides b - f(b), we have f(b) = b + p*k for some integer k. Then f(b)^{p^{k_p}} ≡ (b + p*k)^{p^{k_p}} (mod p^{k_p+1})? Not straightforward.\n\nBut the solution's step 3 seems flawed: they assume they can set f(b)=1 arbitrarily. That's not justified. However, maybe they can choose b such that f(b) = 1 because they can argue that there exists some b with f(b) = 1 (maybe b = 1 is the only one). But they need b to be a primitive root modulo p^{k_p}, which is >1. So they cannot choose b = 1. So they cannot guarantee f(b) = 1.\n\nThus step 3 is suspect. Let's examine if there is any other way to prove that k_p cannot be ≥2. Perhaps there is a different argument. But the solution as given uses an invalid method: they claim \"Taking f(b)=1 (which is allowed because f(1)=1)\" but that is not allowed for arbitrary b. So the reasoning is invalid.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning uses an invalid step: they assume they can assign any value to f(b) arbitrarily