in (\u2605).  We obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\qquad\\forall b .\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(b=1\\) gives \\(f(1)\\mid 1-f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\).  \nIf \\(f(1)>1\\) then the right\u2011hand side is congruent to \\(-1\\pmod{f(1)}\\), impossible.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The value at \\(2\\)\n\nSet \\(a=b=2\\) in (\u2605):\n\n\\[\nf(2)\\mid 4-f(2)^{\\,f(2)} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(2)^{\\,f(2)}\\equiv0\\pmod{f(2)}\\), (2) forces \\(f(2)\\mid4\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(2)\\in\\{1,2,4\\}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Consequences of \\(f(2)=4\\)\n\nAssume from now on that \\(f(2)=4\\) (the only case that can give a ratio larger than \\(1\\)).  \nWith \\(a=2\\) in (\u2605) we obtain for every \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\n4\\mid b^{2}-f(b)^{4}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(4\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb^{2}\\equiv f(b)^{4}\\pmod 4 .\n\\]\n\nSince a square is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) modulo \\(4\\) and a fourth power is \\(0\\) (if the base is even) or \\(1\\) (if the base is odd), (4) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(b)\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(f\\) preserves parity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  An upper bound for all other arguments\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and denote \\(d:=f(n)\\).  \nFrom (\u2605) with \\(a=n,\\;b=n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nd\\mid n^{\\,n}-d^{\\,d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad d\\mid n^{\\,n}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence every prime divisor of \\(d\\) also divides \\(n\\).  \nWrite the prime factorisations  \n\n\\[\nn=\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_i^{e_i},\\qquad \nd=\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_i^{k_i}\\qquad(0\\le k_i\\le e_i n).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow use (\u2605) with \\(a=2,\\;b=n\\).  \nBecause of (5) we have \\(d\\equiv n\\pmod2\\); consequently \\(d\\) and \\(n\\) have the same parity.  \nEquation (4) with \\(b=n\\) reads  \n\n\\[\n4\\mid n^{2}-d^{4}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nReduce (8) modulo \\(p_i^{\\,2}\\) for an arbitrary prime \\(p_i\\mid n\\).\n\n*If \\(p_i\\) is odd.*  \nSince \\(p_i\\mid n\\), we have \\(n\\equiv0\\pmod{p_i}\\) and (8) yields  \n\n\\[\nd^{4}\\equiv0\\pmod{p_i^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(p_i^{2}\\mid d^{4}\\), i.e. \\(p_i\\mid d\\).  \nMoreover the exponent of \\(p_i\\) in \\(d\\) cannot exceed the exponent of \\(p_i\\) in \\(n\\); otherwise \\(d^{4}\\) would be divisible by a higher power of \\(p_i\\) than \\(n^{2}\\), contradicting (8).  \nHence \\(k_i\\le e_i\\).\n\n*If \\(p_i=2\\).*  \nBecause of the parity condition (5) the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(d\\) is the same as that in \\(n\\); again we obtain \\(k_i\\le e_i\\).\n\nThus for every \\(i\\) we have \\(k_i\\le e_i\\).  \nFrom (7) this means \\(d\\mid n\\); together with (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=d\\le n\\qquad\\forall n\\ge3 } .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The global bound\n\nCollecting the possibilities for \\(f(2)\\) from (3) and the estimate (9) we have\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & n=1,\\\\[2pt]\n2, & n=2\\text{ and }f(2)=2,\\\\[2pt]\n4, & n=2\\text{ and }f(2)=4,\\\\[2pt]\nn, & n\\ge3 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence for every bonza function  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le 2n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N } .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Sharpness of the constant\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(n)=n\\ \\ (n\\ge3).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n* For \\(a=1\\) the condition is trivial because \\(f(1)=1\\).  \n* For \\(a=2\\) we need \\(4\\mid b^{2}-f(b)^{4}\\).  \n  By (5) the right\u2011hand side is \\(0\\pmod4\\) for every \\(b\\):  \n  \u2013 if \\(b\\) is even, both \\(b^{2}\\) and \\(f(b)^{4}\\) are multiples of \\(4\\);  \n  \u2013 if \\(b\\) is odd, both are congruent to \\(1\\pmod4\\).  \n* For \\(a\\ge3\\) we