"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that every bonza function satisfies  \n\\[\nf(n)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\in\\mathbb N .\n\\]\nConsequently the smallest real constant \\(c\\) with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **First Lemma \u2013 the value at\u202f1.**  \n   Show that a bonza function must have \\(f(1)=1\\).  \n   The condition with \\(a=1\\) forces the congruence  \n   \\[\n   b\\equiv f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\pmod{f(1)}\\qquad(\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N).\n   \\]\n   If \\(f(1)=k\\ge 2\\) we obtain a contradiction by examining the cases\n   \\(b=k\\) and \\(b=k+1\\); hence \\(k=1\\).\n\n2. **Second Lemma \u2013 a prime divisor of a value propagates.**  \n   Let \\(p\\) be a prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\).  \n   Using the defining condition with \\((a,b)=(n,p)\\) we get  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-f(p)^{\\,f(n)} .\n   \\]\n   Reducing modulo \\(p\\) yields \\(p\\mid f(p)\\).  \n   By induction (see step\u202f3) we will have \\(f(p)=p\\).\n\n3. **Induction on \\(n\\).**  \n   The statement \\(f(m)\\le m\\) is true for \\(m=1\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   Assume it holds for all integers \\(<n\\) and suppose, to obtain a\n   contradiction, that \\(f(n)>n\\).  \n   Let \\(p\\) be any prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\).  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(p\\mid f(p)\\) and, by the induction hypothesis,\n   \\(f(p)\\le p\\); therefore \\(f(p)=p\\).\n\n   Substituting \\(f(p)=p\\) into the divisibility obtained in Lemma\u202f2 gives  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-p^{\\,f(n)}=p^{\\,n}\\bigl(p^{\\,f(n)-n}-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\gcd\\!\\bigl(p^{\\,n},\\,p^{\\,f(n)-n}-1\\bigr)=1\\), the divisor\n   \\(f(n)\\) must divide the second factor:\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid p^{\\,f(n)-n}-1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Put \\(t:=f(n)-n\\;(t\\ge 1)\\).  Because \\(p\\ge 2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   p^{\\,t}-1 < p^{\\,t}\\le 2^{\\,t}.\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain \\(f(n)\\le p^{\\,t}-1<2^{\\,t}\\).  On the other hand,\n   \\(f(n)=n+t\\ge t+1\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   t+1\\le f(n)<2^{\\,t}.\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality is impossible for any integer \\(t\\ge 1\\):\n   for \\(t=1\\) it would give \\(2\\le f(n)<2\\), and for \\(t\\ge 2\\) we have\n   \\(t+1\\ge 3\\) while \\(2^{\\,t}\\le t+1\\) fails (easily checked for\n   \\(t=2,3,\\dots\\)).  Thus the assumption \\(f(n)>n\\) cannot hold.\n\n   Consequently \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for every \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) satisfies the bonza condition, so the\n   bound \\(f(n)\\le n\\) is best possible.  Therefore the smallest constant\n   with the required property is \\(c=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a bonza function, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N } \\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  \\(f(1)=1\\).\n\nPut \\(a=1\\) in (\u2605).  Denoting \\(k:=f(1)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,k}\\qquad(\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge 2\\) choose \\(b=k\\).  Then (1) gives \\(k\\mid k-f(k)^{\\,k}\\), whence\n\\(k\\mid f(k)^{\\,k}\\); consequently every prime divisor of \\(k\\) divides\n\\(f(k)\\).  Let \\(p\\) be such a prime.  From (1) with \\(b=k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\mid (k+1)-f(k+1)^{\\,k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(k+1)^{\\,k}\\equiv 1\\pmod p .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\mid k\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\), the exponent \\(k\\) is a multiple of\n\\(p-1\\); thus \\(f(k+1)\\) is coprime to \\(p\\).  But from (1) with\n\\(b=k\\) we already know \\(p\\mid f(k)\\).  Applying (\u2605) with\n\\((a,b)=(k,p)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(k)\\mid p^{\\,k}-f(p)^{\\,f(k)} .\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(p\\) gives \\(p\\mid f(p)^{\\,f(k)}\\), so \\(p\\mid f(p)\\).\nNow use (\u2605) with \\((a,b)=(p,k)\\):\n\n\\[\nf(p)\\mid k^{\\,p}-f(k)^{\\,f(p)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\mid f(p)\\) and \\(p\\mid f(k)\\), the right\u2013hand side is\ncongruent to \\(-f(k)^{\\,f(p)}\\equiv0\\pmod p\\); hence \\(p\\mid k\\), a\ncontradiction because \\(p\\) already divides \\(k\\).  Therefore the\nassumption \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible and we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Propagation of prime divisors.\n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\).  Apply (\u2605) with \\((a,b)=(n,p)\\):\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-f(p)^{\\,f(n)} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nReducing (2) modulo \\(p\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n0\\equiv -f(p)^{\\,f(n)}\\pmod p\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\mid f(p). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3.  Induction bound \\(f(m)\\le m\\).\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\) that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(m)\\le m\\qquad(\\forall m\\in\\mathbb N)} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Base.*  For \\(m=1\\) this is Lemma\u202f1.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume (4) holds for all integers \\(<n\\) and suppose,\nto the contrary, that \\(f(n)>n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nt:=f(n)-n\\qquad(t\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nChoose a prime divisor \\(p\\) of \\(f(n)\\).  By Lemma\u202f2, (3) gives\n\\(p\\mid f(p)\\); the induction hypothesis applied to \\(p<n\\) yields\n\\(f(p)\\le p\\), whence \\(f(p)=p\\).\n\nInsert \\(f(p)=p\\) into (2):\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-p^{\\,f(n)}=p^{\\,n}\\bigl(p^{\\,t}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd\\!\\bigl(p^{\\,n},\\,p^{\\,t}-1\\bigr)=1\\), the divisor \\(f(n)\\)\nmust divide the second factor:\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid p^{\\,t}-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\np^{\\,t}-1< p^{\\,t}\\le 2^{\\,t}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le p^{\\,t}-1<2^{\\,t}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(f(n)=n+t\\ge t+1\\); combining with (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nt+1\\le f(n)<2^{\\,t}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(t=1\\) this would read \\(2\\le f(n)<2\\), impossible.\nFor \\(t\\ge2\\) we have \\(2^{\\,t}\\le t+1\\) is false (easily verified for\n\\(t=2,3,\\dots\\)).  Hence no such \\(t\\) exists, contradicting the\nassumption \\(f(n)>n\\).\n\nTherefore the induction step is proved, and (4) holds for every\n\\(n\\in\\mathbb N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nThe identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) satisfies (\u2605) because\n\n\\[\nn\\mid b^{\\,n}-b^{\\,n}=0\\qquad(\\forall a=n,\\;b\\in\\mathbb N).\n\\]\n\nThus the bound \\(f(n)\\le n\\) is best possible.  Consequently the smallest\nreal constant \\(c\\) such that every bonza function obeys \\(f(n)\\le cn\\)\nfor all \\(n\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
