\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a *bonza* function, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\text{for all }a,b\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag{*}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A first divisibility\n\nPut \\(b=a\\) in \\((*)\\). Since the second term on the right is a multiple of \\(f(a)\\),\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\boxed{\\,f(a)\\mid a^{a}\\,}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus every value of \\(f\\) is a divisor of a pure power of its argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Odd primes\n\nLet \\(p\\) be an odd prime. From (1) we have \\(f(p)=p^{\\alpha}\\) for some\n\\(\\alpha\\ge 0\\) with \\(\\alpha\\le p\\).\n\nTake \\(a=p,\\;b=2\\) in \\((*)\\). Using \\(f(2)\\mid 2^{2}=4\\) we have\n\\(f(2)\\in\\{1,2,4\\}\\). The condition reads\n\n\\[\np^{\\alpha}\\mid 2^{p}-f(2)^{\\,p^{\\alpha}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\) then the left–hand side is divisible by \\(p\\). Reducing\n(2) modulo \\(p\\) gives\n\n\\[\n0\\equiv 2^{p}-f(2)^{\\,p^{\\alpha}}\\pmod p .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is odd, Fermat’s theorem yields \\(2^{p}\\equiv2\\pmod p\\).\nMoreover \\(f(2)^{\\,p^{\\alpha}}\\equiv f(2)^{\\,p}\\equiv f(2)^{\\,p}\\pmod p\\).\nSince \\(f(2)\\in\\{1,2,4\\}\\) and none of these numbers is congruent to \\(2\\)\nmodulo an odd prime, the congruence cannot hold. Hence \\(\\alpha=0\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(p)=1\\qquad\\text{for every odd prime }p.} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Reduction to the power of two\n\nWrite any integer uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nn=2^{k}m,\\qquad k\\ge0,\\;m\\text{ odd}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we know that \\(f(n)\\) divides \\(n^{n}=2^{kn}m^{n}\\); consequently\n\\(f(n)\\) is a power of \\(2\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{t}\\qquad(t\\ge0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Restriction coming from odd bases\n\nLet \\(b\\) be an odd integer. By (3) we have \\(f(b)=1\\).  \nInsert \\((a,b)=(n,b)\\) into \\((*)\\); using (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2^{t}\\mid b^{\\,n}-1\\qquad\\forall\\;b\\text{ odd}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed odd \\(b\\) the multiplicative order of \\(b\\) modulo \\(2^{t}\\)\nis \\(2^{t-2}\\) (standard property of odd residues modulo powers of two).\nThus (5) forces  \n\n\\[\n2^{t-2}\\mid n=2^{k}m\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t\\le k+2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  No further restriction from the base \\(2\\)\n\nTake \\(b=2\\) in \\((*)\\).  Because of (1) we have \\(f(2)\\mid 2^{2}=4\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(2)\\in\\{1,2,4\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWith \\((a,b)=(n,2)\\) and using (4) we get\n\n\\[\n2^{t}\\mid 2^{\\,n}-f(2)^{\\,2^{t}}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(f(2)=2^{s}\\) with \\(s\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  The right–hand side of (8) equals  \n\n\\[\n2^{\\min (n,\\,2s)}\\bigl(2^{|n-2s|}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe factor in parentheses is odd; therefore the exact power of \\(2\\) dividing\nthe right–hand side is \\(2^{\\min (n,\\,2s)}\\).  Condition (8) is thus\nequivalent to  \n\n\\[\nt\\le \\min (n,\\,2s). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n=2^{k}m\\ge 2^{k}\\) and \\(s\\le2\\), we have \\(\\min (n,2s)=2s\\ge t\\)\nwhenever \\(t\\le k+2\\) (recall that \\(k\\ge0\\)).  Consequently (9) imposes\nno restriction beyond (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Universal bound\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(t\\le k+2\\).  Using (4),\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{t}\\le 2^{k+2}=4\\cdot2^{k}\\le 4\\cdot2^{k}m =4n.\n\\]\n\nHence every bonza function satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(n)\\le 4n\\quad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N.} \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) by  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\begin{cases}\n4\\cdot2^{k} &\\text{if }n=2^{k}m\\text{ with }k\\ge1\\;(m\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\n1           &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd }(>1),\\\\[2mm]\n1           &\\text{if }n=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIn words, \\(f(n)=4\\) times the highest power of \\(2\\) dividing \\(n\\); for\nodd numbers we set \\(f=1\\).\n\n*Verification of the bonza condition.*\n\n* (i)  For odd \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)=1\\); then \\((*)\\) becomes\n\\(f(a)\\mid b^{a}-1\\).  By (6) the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(f(a)\\) does not\nexceed \\(k+2\\), and the order of any odd base modulo \\(2^{k+2}\\) divides\n\\(2^{k}\\), so the divisibility holds.\n\n* (ii) For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(f(2)=4\\).  Let \\(a=2^{k}m\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).\nUsing (8) with \\(t=k+2\\) and \\(s=2\\) we obtain\n\\(2^{k+2}\\mid 2^{\\,a}-4^{\\,2^{k+2}}\\), which is true because the right‑hand\nside equals \\(2^{k+2}\\bigl(2^{a-2(k+2)}-1\\bigr)\\).\n\n* (iii) For two even arguments \\(a=2^{k}m,\\;b=2^{\\ell}n\\) the condition\nreduces to a divisibility between powers of \\(2\\):\n\\(2^{k+2}\\mid 2^{\\ell a}-2^{(\\ell+2)2^{k+2}}\\).  The exponent of the\nleft‑hand side is \\(k+2\\), while the right‑hand side contains the factor\n\\(2^{\\ell a}\\) with \\(\\ell a\\ge k+2\\); the remaining factor is odd, so the\ndivisibility holds.\n\nThus the function defined by (11) is bonza.  For \\(n=4\\) we have\n\\(f(4)=16\\), giving \\(f(4)=4\\cdot4\\).  Consequently the constant in (10)\ncannot be reduced below \\(4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer\n\nThe smallest real constant \\(c\\) such that every bonza function satisfies\n\\(f(n)\\le cn\\) for all positive integers \\(n\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]"
