od p), so 2 - 2^p ≡ 0 (mod p). Actually if f(2)=2, then f(2)^{p^α} ≡ 2^p (mod p) because exponent p^α ≡ p (mod p-1?) Wait, we need to consider exponent modulo p-1? Actually we are reducing modulo p, but exponent matters because we are raising f(2) to a power. Since f(2) is an integer, we can use Fermat's little theorem: For any integer a not divisible by p, a^{p-1} ≡ 1 (mod p). So a^{k} mod p depends on k mod (p-1). So we need to compute f(2)^{p^α} mod p. Since p is odd prime, p does not divide f(2) (since f(2) ∈ {1,2,4} and p≥3). So we can reduce exponent modulo p-1: p^α mod (p-1). Since p ≡ 1 (mod p-1), p^α ≡ 1^α ≡ 1 (mod p-1). So f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2)^1 ≡ f(2) (mod p). So indeed f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2) (mod p). So RHS ≡ 2^p - f(2) (mod p). Since 2^p ≡ 2 (mod p), RHS ≡ 2 - f(2) (mod p). So we need 2 - f(2) ≡ 0 (mod p) => f(2) ≡ 2 (mod p). Since f(2) ∈ {1,2,4}, only f(2)=2 works for any p? Actually 2 ≡ 2 (mod p) always true, because p divides 2-2 = 0. So for any odd prime p, if f(2)=2, the congruence holds. But the solution claims none of these numbers (1,2,4) are congruent to 2 mod p for odd p. That's false: f(2)=2 is congruent to 2 mod p for any p. So the argument is flawed. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe condition is p^α | 2^p - f(2)^{p^α}. If α≥1, p divides RHS. The congruence mod p yields 2^p - f(2)^{p^α} ≡ 2 - f(2) (mod p). So we need 2 - f(2) ≡ 0 (mod p). This holds if f(2) = 2. So the solution's claim that \"none of these numbers is congruent to 2 modulo an odd prime\" is false. Indeed, f(2)=2 is congruent to 2 mod p for any p. So the argument that α must be 0 is invalid. However, we need to check if there is any other condition that might rule out f(2)=2? Let's check the original condition for a=p, b=2: p^α | 2^p - f(2)^{p^α}. If f(2)=2 and α≥1, then RHS = 2^p - 2^{p^α} = 2^p (1 - 2^{p^α - p}). Since α≥1, p^α ≥ p (since α≥1). Actually p^α is p raised to α, not p times α. So p^α grows quickly; for α=1, p^α = p, so RHS = 2^p - 2^p = 0, so p^α divides 0, which is