tion: the order of q modulo p^α must divide β p^α - p. Since q is arbitrary odd prime, this can't hold for all q unless α=0. Indeed, for α≥1, there exist q such that q is a primitive root modulo p^α (if p is odd prime, there exists primitive root modulo p^α). Then order of q modulo p^α is φ(p^α) = p^{α-1}(p-1). For the condition to hold for that q, we need p^{α-1}(p-1) | β p^α - p = p (β p^{α-1} - 1). This implies p^{α-1}(p-1) divides p (β p^{α-1} - 1). Since p^{α-1} divides RHS, we need (p-1) | β p^{α-1} - 1. This is possible for some β? For given p, α, we could choose β such that β p^{α-1} ≡ 1 (mod p-1). Since p^{α-1} ≡ 1 (mod p-1), this reduces to β ≡ 1 (mod p-1). So choose β = 1 + k(p-1). Since β must be ≤ q (since f(q) = q^β ≤ q^q), we can choose β=1. So if β=1, then condition becomes q^{p^α - p} ≡ 1 (mod p^α). For primitive root q, order is p^{α-1}(p-1). So we need p^{α-1}(p-1) | p^α - p = p(p^{α-1} - 1). Since p^{α-1}(p-1) divides p(p^{α-1} - 1) only if (p-1) divides p(p^{α-1} - 1). Since p-1 and p are coprime, we need (p-1) | (p^{α-1} - 1). This holds for α=1 (p^{0} - 1 = 0). For α>1, p^{α-1} ≡ 1 (mod p-1) by Fermat's little theorem (since p ≡ 1 (mod p-1), p^{α-1} ≡ 1^{α-1} ≡ 1 (mod p-1)). So p^{α-1} - 1 ≡ 0 (mod p-1). So (p-1) divides p^{α-1} - 1. So condition holds for any α≥1. So for primitive root q, the condition q^{p^α - p} ≡ 1 (mod p^α) holds? Actually we need to check the exponent modulo φ(p^α) = p^{α-1}(p-1). Since q is primitive root, q^{k} ≡ 1 (mod p^α) iff p^{α-1}(p-1) | k. So we need p^{α-1}(p-1) | p^α - p = p(p^{α-1} - 1). Since p^{α-1} - 1 is divisible by (p-1) as shown, we have p(p^{α-1} - 1) = p(p-1) * something? Let's compute: p^{α-1} - 1 = (p-1)(p^{α-2} + p^{α-3} + ... + 1). So p(p^{α-1} - 1) = p(p-1)(p^{α-2} + ... + 1). So p(p-1) times something. But we need p^{α-1}(p-1) dividing that. Since p^{α-1}(p-1) = p^{α-1} * (p-1). Compare p(p-1)*(something) vs p^{α-1}(p-1). Cancel (p-1). Need p^{α-1} | p * (p^{α-2} + ... + 1). The factor (p^{α-2}