orem gives 2^p ≡ 2 (mod p). Also f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2)^p (mod p) because exponent p^α ≡ p (mod p-1?) Wait: For modulo p, we need to reduce exponent modulo p-1 due to FLT for base not divisible by p. However f(2) is 1,2, or 4, none divisible by p (p odd prime >2). So we can reduce exponent modulo p-1: f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2)^{p^α mod (p-1)} (mod p). Since p is odd prime, p-1 is even. But they claim f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2)^p (mod p). That's not necessarily correct. Let's examine.\n\nWe have exponent e = p^α. For any integer a not divisible by p, a^{p-1} ≡ 1 (mod p). So a^{e} ≡ a^{e mod (p-1)} (mod p). So we need to compute e mod (p-1). Since p^α mod (p-1) =? p ≡ 1 (mod p-1). So p^α ≡ 1^α ≡ 1 (mod p-1). Indeed, because p ≡ 1 (mod p-1), raising to any power yields 1 mod (p-1). So p^α ≡ 1 (mod p-1). So a^{p^α} ≡ a^1 ≡ a (mod p). So f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2) (mod p). The solution incorrectly says f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2)^p (mod p). But they later claim that none of f(2) ∈ {1,2,4} is congruent to 2 mod p for odd p. Let's compute correctly: The congruence needed is 2^p - f(2)^{p^α} ≡ 0 (mod p). Using FLT, 2^p ≡ 2 (mod p). Using exponent reduction, f(2)^{p^α} ≡ f(2) (mod p). So condition becomes 2 - f(2) ≡ 0 (mod p), i.e., f(2) ≡ 2 (mod p). Since p > 2, f(2) ∈ {1,2,4}. Only f(2)=2 satisfies this congruence. So the condition would be possible for any odd prime p if f(2)=2. But the solution says none of these numbers is congruent to 2 mod p, which is false for f(2)=2 (2 ≡ 2 mod p). So the argument fails: It incorrectly eliminates the case α ≥ 1 for any odd prime p, concluding f(p)=1. But we need to check if there is a possibility that f(p) = p^α with α ≥ 1 and f(2)=2 works.\n\nLet's test: Suppose f(2)=2. Then for odd prime p, condition p^α | 2^p - 2^{p^α} = 2^{p^α} (2^{p-p^α} - 1). Since p > 2, p does not divide 2. So p^α dividing 2^{p^α} (2^{p-p^α} - 1). The factor 2^{p^α} is a power of 2, not divisible by p. So p^α must divide (2^{p-p^α} - 1). But p^α > p (if α≥2) or p^α = p (if α=1). Let's examine α=1: p